+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+#   The Android app builder's two actions in the tool->action table
+#   (jarvis_agent.py's `android_app` tool, DEF-068, 2026-11-14)
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# THEY ARE HERE, AT THE END OF THE FILE, for the reason the two `_RISK.update`
+# blocks above give: every line inside `_TOOL_ACTIONS` is some earlier patch's
+# hunk context, and the two entries this adds would land between
+# web-search-grant.patch's own added lines and the closing brace - which broke
+# that patch's reversal the moment they were typed in there (measured on this
+# branch: "error: patch failed: jarvis_gate.py:1052"). The end of the file
+# belongs to no hunk in either tree. `_TOOL_ACTIONS.update` runs at import,
+# before any `check()` call, so `action_for_tool()` answers these two exactly as
+# if they were in the literal.
+#
+# Why they are needed at all: `android_app` puts its build and install halves to
+# the gate under the builder module's OWN action names
+# (jarvis_android_builder.BUILD_ACTION / INSTALL_ACTION), so each name maps to
+# itself - the shape "send_file_to_phone" above uses. Without them
+# `action_for_tool()` answers "unclassified_tool": the Reach page calls both
+# unclassified and the card shown is not the one the tool raises. Both actions
+# already carry their risky `_RISK` rows, their `_NO_RULE_FROM_DENIAL` membership
+# and their "ask" lines in both shipped settings copies; these two lines are only
+# what makes the question answerable. The Android builder itself shipped in the
+# base with NO patch (2026-10-30), so on a live install these arrive the same way
+# its `_RISK` rows did - see tools/repair_live_backend_gaps.py - and
+# jarvis_agent._one_call passes the action name through as a belt until they do.
+_TOOL_ACTIONS.update({
+    "app_build_android": "app_build_android",
+    "app_install_android": "app_install_android",
+})
+
